## Lemon Law Coverage for Pre-Owned Vehicles

In California, the protections of lemon law extend to used vehicles that are sold with warranties provided by dealerships.

## Understanding Warranties on Used Vehicles from Dealerships

When purchasing a used vehicle from a dealer in California, the vehicle often comes with a warranty. Typically, this warranty is valid for 30 days after your purchase or for the first 1,000 miles you drive, whichever milestone is reached first.

Should your pre-owned vehicle exhibit problems that align with the criteria of CA lemon law during this warranty period, it may be eligible for the same protections as a new vehicle under lemon law CA considerations.

Your Rights as a Consumer Under California Lemon Law

Dealerships bear the responsibility of either repairing a defective used vehicle or initiating a buyback. In cases related to Coronado lemon law, the costs associated with legal proceedings are generally borne by the entity that provided the warranty.

The Implications of Buying a Vehicle “As Is”

Purchasing a vehicle “as is” signifies that you accept all subsequent issues the vehicle may have post-purchase, which could limit your protections under Coronado lemon law.

However, it’s important to note that for an “as is” purchase to be legally binding, this condition must be clearly indicated and visible on the buyer’s guide while the vehicle is on display at the dealership. If this information was not adequately communicated, you may still have grounds for a claim under California lemon law.

