## Frequently Asked Questions About CA Lemon Law

## Do I Qualify for California Lemon Law?

To qualify for California lemon law, your vehicle must have been purchased with a warranty - of which was still valid when the defect was first reported to the manufacturer.

Typically, these warranties last for 3 years from the date of purchase or up to 36,000 miles, whichever occurs first - this varies based on the auto brand.

The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty. In reality, you can pursue a lemon law claim at any point during the warranty period, though claims filed within the initial 18-month/18,000-mile timeframe often carry more weight.

## Can I File a Claim Without Legal Representation?

While it’s possible to file a lemon law claim independently, it’s important to be aware that manufacturers frequently dismiss claims made directly by consumers. These large corporations have various tactics to avoid responsibility for selling defective vehicles.

Partnering with a seasoned California lemon law attorney can be pivotal in [effectively negotiating](/blog/tips-for-negotiating-with-car-dealers-in-a-lemon-case) and achieving the compensation you rightfully deserve.

## Is There a Statute of Limitations for Filing a Claim?

In California, there is a four-year statute of limitations for filing lemon law claims. Prompt filing is crucial, as delays might imply that the defect in question did not significantly impact your usage of the vehicle. Additionally, continuing to operate a defective vehicle can pose safety risks to you and others on the road.

## Should I Opt for a Buyback or Replacement?

When dealing with a lemon vehicle, you may have a preference for a replacement vehicle from the same manufacturer, typically of the same model or equivalent value.

However, manufacturers are only legally required to offer a buyback and may not always agree to a replacement. In most cases, opting for a buyback is the recommended course of action for lemon vehicles.

## What Are the Lemon Law Benefits in Private Car Sales?

California lemon law does not require private sellers to offer warranties on vehicles. Most private sales are conducted under an “as is” basis, which generally leaves little legal recourse if the vehicle breaks down shortly after purchase, even with the support of a Coronado lemon law attorney.

## What is the Settlement Timeline for Lemon Law Cases?

The time it takes to resolve a lemon law case in California can vary widely. While the aim is to settle cases within 30 days, achieving this is not always possible. The specific timeline of a lemon law claim hinges on various factors, each case having its own unique set of challenges and details.
